course: dictionary
lesson: 7319
title: Plagiarism: your work, your words
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Plagiarism means copying someone else's words, ideas or work and pretending they are your own. It is a kind of cheating. «Copying text and passing it off as your own is plagiarism, a kind of cheating.» Passing it off means showing it to people as if you wrote it.
explain: Copying does not have to be a whole page. Using someone's exact sentence, or their clever idea, without saying where it came from is still plagiarism. «Copyright is a law that protects creators' work; plagiarism is copying someone's work and saying it's yours.» Copyright is the law that protects the work, and plagiarism is the cheating.
explain: To avoid plagiarism, write in your own words and say where the facts came from. This is called giving the source. Copying an AI's answer and pretending you wrote it is plagiarism too.
fact: Plagiarism is copying someone else's words, ideas or work and pretending they are your own.
fact: You can commit plagiarism, avoid plagiarism or check for plagiarism.
fact: Plagiarism is a kind of cheating.
fact: To avoid it, write in your own words and say where the information came from.
fact: Copying an AI's answer and pretending you wrote it is plagiarism too.
exercise: «Copying text and passing it off as your own is plagiarism.» What is plagiarism? | Asking a teacher for help | Writing your own words and giving the source | *Copying someone else's work and pretending it is yours | Reading a book quietly
exercise: Ella copies a fact from a website into her project and writes nothing about where it came from. What should she do? | Copy it again, word for word, with no changes | Hide the website so nobody can check | *Write it in her own words and give the website as her source | Leave the project blank
exercise: «Copying someone's writing and pretending it is yours is called plagiarism.» Which word names this? | Copyright | Context | *Plagiarism | Synonym
test: Jayden copies an AI's answer and hands it in as his own work. What is this? | Context clues | Good research | *Plagiarism, a kind of cheating
test: Which is the best way to avoid plagiarism? | *Write in your own words and say where the facts came from | Copy the whole page and say nothing | Hide where you found the facts | Copy the text and change one word
exam: Pupil asks, «Is it plagiarism if I copy one sentence and say which book it came from?» | *No, you are not pretending it is yours, because you give the source | Yes, but only if the book is old | Yes, copying is always plagiarism, even if you give the source
